From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sahil Faizal <sahil.faizal@amd.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 05:00:00 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] clr: Publish thread state with an atomic

HostcallListener termination polls Thread::state while its worker calls
setState. The volatile enum does not synchronize these accesses, and the
instrumented HIP tests report a host data race between the two methods.

Use an atomic enum. Keep the existing state transitions and the default
sequentially consistent loads/stores so state publication also orders the
associated thread data. All state writes already go through setState.

Source: ROCm/rocm-systems bd1130f9f838cfffd24f41fd3052968fb7cae4e0
Reproduction: https://github.com/ROCm/TheRock/actions/runs/43015193286
Carry temporarily in TheRock pending upstream CLR review.
---
 projects/clr/rocclr/thread/thread.hpp | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/projects/clr/rocclr/thread/thread.hpp b/projects/clr/rocclr/thread/thread.hpp
--- a/projects/clr/rocclr/thread/thread.hpp
+++ b/projects/clr/rocclr/thread/thread.hpp
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
 #include "top.hpp"
 #include "os/os.hpp"
 
+#include <atomic>
 #include <string>
 
 #if defined(_WIN32)
@@ -41,7 +42,7 @@ class Thread {
   //! The thread's name.
   const std::string name_;
   //! Current running state.
-  volatile ThreadState state_;
+  std::atomic<ThreadState> state_;
   //! The argument passed to run()
   void* data_;
 
